    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d532">

                <pb facs="0524" n="512" sameAs="frus:frus1943v04/pg512"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.20/187</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in Iran</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dreyfus</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 191</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-02-11">February 11,
                        1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Reference is made to your telegram no. 81,
                    January 23, 1943 regarding the proposal of the Iranian Government that Major
                    General <pb facs="0525" n="513" xml:id="pg513"/> Clarence S. Ridley should be
                    appointed Assistant Minister of War of Iran. The substance of your telegram was
                    communicated to the War Department, which also received word direct from General
                    Ridley.</p>
                <p>The War Department has now informed the Department that it would prefer to have
                    General Ridley remain for the time being in his present status as an adviser and
                    does not wish to acquiesce in his appointment as Assistant Minister of War. In
                    making this decision, the War Department is believed to have been influenced by
                    a fear that by accepting an official position in the Iranian Government, General
                    Ridley might become subject to criticism, from Iranian and other sources, in
                    connection with Iranian Army affairs which he would not have sufficient
                    authority to control, since he would be subordinate to the Minister of War, the
                    Prime Minister, and the Shah. The Department understands, however, that the War
                    Department might be willing to reconsider its decision provided the Iranian
                    Government feels strongly that the appointment is essential and you and General
                    Ridley concur.</p>
                <p>There is enclosed a copy of a self-explanatory memorandum of a conversation with
                    the Iranian Minister regarding this matter.<anchor n="96" xml:id="d532-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sumner Welles</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
